<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE policyconfig PUBLIC
 "-//freedesktop//DTD PolicyKit Policy Configuration 1.0//EN"
 "http://www.freedesktop.org/standards/PolicyKit/1.0/policyconfig.dtd">
<!--
  SPDX-License-Identifier: Apache-2.0

  Why this exists. The desktop app holds the READ-ONLY API token, because an AI
  coding agent runs as the same user and a full-scope token in that user's reach
  would let the agent turn enforcement off. When somebody using the app changes
  enforcement, the app runs the equivalent `rz` command through pkexec instead
  of holding a privileged token of its own.

  auth_admin, not auth_admin_keep, and certainly not yes. "yes" would let
  anything running as the user change kernel enforcement with no authentication
  at all, which is precisely the agent we are containing. auth_admin_keep would
  remember the answer for a few minutes, and that retention window is exactly
  when an agent is running alongside you: it could ride an authentication a
  human gave for something else. So every privileged change asks again. That is
  more friction, and the friction is the product.

  To relax it on your own machines without patching this file, which an upgrade
  would overwrite, drop a rules file into /etc/polkit-1/rules.d/ — there is a
  commented example at policies/49-ringzero-relax-auth.rules in the source.

  The exec.path annotation pins this action to one binary. Without it, pkexec
  falls back to the generic org.freedesktop.policykit.exec action and the
  person authenticating sees a generic message about running a program as root
  rather than what is actually about to change.
-->
<policyconfig>

  <vendor>Ring Zero Security</vendor>
  <vendor_url>https://github.com/ringzerosec/jev-runtime-security</vendor_url>
  <icon_name>ringzero-app</icon_name>

  <action id="com.ringzerosecurity.app.run-rz">
    <description>Change Ring Zero Security enforcement</description>
    <description xml:lang="en_GB">Change Ring Zero Security enforcement</description>
    <message>Authentication is required to change what Ring Zero Security blocks on this machine: file access rules, enforcement posture, or a review label.</message>
    <message xml:lang="en_GB">Authentication is required to change what Ring Zero Security blocks on this machine: file access rules, enforcement posture, or a review label.</message>
    <defaults>
      <allow_any>auth_admin</allow_any>
      <allow_inactive>auth_admin</allow_inactive>
      <allow_active>auth_admin</allow_active>
    </defaults>
    <annotate key="org.freedesktop.policykit.exec.path">/usr/bin/rz</annotate>
    <annotate key="org.freedesktop.policykit.exec.allow_gui">true</annotate>
  </action>

</policyconfig>
